## Context

SPEC.md §5.2.3–§5.2.5 is finalized and the three scheduling constants are
ratified (DECISIONS.md, 2026-09-01): weighted round robin r = 3, the ~1 s
micro-batch quantum with per-device size derivation and a claim TTL of
30 × the quantum, and the extract worker's latency-observed back-off.
Implementation starts. The ledger's staged decoupling is what makes the
tranches independently useful: each lands alone, none commits the next.

The TypeScript lands on the author's fork (FORK_REPOSITORY in UPSTREAM),
one fork branch per tranche; these tickets track scope and evidence here.
**From 0568 (2026-09-01) those branches are cut from `conductor-integration`,
not from tranche 2**: the tranches stopped being sequential at tranche 3, the
two siblings that resulted cost one `ledger.ts` merge, and a second pair
running at once would cost another. Branch per ticket still; different base.
Upstream filing is governed by GOVERNANCE.md and is NOT part of this
tracker — the fork accumulates until an upstream PR is prepared
deliberately.

A Python discrete-event simulation was considered and rejected (author,
2026-09-01): it would prove properties of a model that drifts from the
TypeScript. Validation is the real scheduler run in virtual time instead —
the injected clock plus the synthetic library, asserted as fast property
tests, red first.

## Children

- 0551 — ledger schema, reconcile tick, the three fixtures
- 0552 — conductor election and lease machinery
- 0553 — extract shim, class order, latency back-off
- 0554 — chunk and embed workers, quantum autotune, sidecar
- 0555 — fresh/backfill interleave and the scheduling property suite
- 0556 — the v2 content schema (entries/slabs/passages/FTS) 0554 needs;
  not a child tranche, filed alongside once the gap surfaced
- 0565 — wires seg/1 (ticket 0028) into the conductor's write loop;
  filed once "chunk worker over stored text" turned out to assume
  entries already exist, with no ticket owning how they get there
- 0566 — assembles tick+conductor+extract+chunk/embed into one runnable
  process, and is the ticket that actually answers "point at a fixture
  library and index it"; none of 0551-0556/0565 do that alone
- 0568 — one integration base: 0553's and 0556's fork branches are
  divergent siblings that conflict in `ledger.ts`, and 0554, 0565 and
  0566 all assume one tree. Filed by the raid on 0565/0566; not a
  tranche, the same way 0556 was not
- 0575 — the embedding service boundary the 2026-09-02 topology ruled and
  nobody owned: 0491 owns which candidate provides the runtime, the
  registry tickets own the entry it serves, 0554 owns the caller. Not a
  tranche; not Blocked-by 0491, and its body says why
- 0576 — probe: can a Claude-Desktop-hosted server spawn a child at all,
  and under what environment. Gates 0566
- 0577 — probe: the embedding service ceiling sized for two resident
  generations. Gates 0566

Children chain by Blocked-by on their real prerequisite, never on this
tracker. Invariant across every tranche: the incumbent MiniLM entry
reproduces its old vectors and keys byte for byte, and upstream's own
test suite stays green on the fork branch.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] All five children closed (0551-0555; 0556 is not a tranche, see
      Children)
- [ ] The 2026-09-02 topology is carried in the children's BODIES and not
      only in their logs: the writer as its own process (0566), a worker
      that holds no model and runs under a bound (0554), and an owner for
      the embedding service boundary (0575)
- [ ] The two probes the ruling demands before 0566 commits the boundary
      (0576, 0577) are answered and recorded, and a negative on either
      goes to DECISIONS.md as a question rather than into code as a
      workaround
- [ ] Integration review: full suite on the fork's assembled branch,
      scheduling properties green, byte-identical vector check green —
      the last of which had no test at all until 0554 built one (0568)
